<Site title="Why AI crawlers can't read your JavaScript site — CiteRank" description="Googlebot renders JavaScript. GPTBot, ClaudeBot and PerplexityBot don't. Here's what that means for your rankings in AI search.">
  <section class="pagehead">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Technical · 5 min read</span>
      <h1>Why AI crawlers can't read your JavaScript site</h1>
      <p>Your page can rank perfectly on Google and be invisible to ChatGPT at the exact same time. Here's the crawler behavior behind it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <main>
    <article class="post wrap">
      <p><strong>Googlebot executes JavaScript. GPTBot, ClaudeBot and PerplexityBot do not.</strong> That single asymmetry is responsible for more AEO failures than any content-quality issue — and it's completely invisible unless you go looking for it, because your page looks fine in a browser and ranks fine on Google.</p>

      <h2>How this actually plays out</h2>
      <p>A modern e-commerce or SPA-style site often ships a near-empty HTML shell — a single <code>&lt;div id="root"&gt;</code> — and lets client-side JavaScript render the product name, price, description and reviews after the page loads. A human visitor never notices. Googlebot runs a full Chromium instance to render the page before indexing it, so it never notices either.</p>
      <p>But GPTBot, ClaudeBot and PerplexityBot fetch the raw HTML only — the same bytes the server sent before any script ran. If your content is injected by JavaScript, these crawlers receive exactly what's in that empty shell: nothing. No product name, no price, no description. As far as ChatGPT or Perplexity is concerned, the page doesn't exist.</p>

      <h2>Why this is worse than it sounds</h2>
      <p>It's not a partial degradation — it's binary. A page that's 80% JavaScript-rendered doesn't get 80% of an AI citation; it gets none, because there's nothing left to quote. And because the failure mode is silent (no error, no broken page, just an absence), most site owners never find out until they think to check — which is precisely the gap a dedicated crawler-access check exists to close.</p>

      <h2>How to check your own site</h2>
      <p>Compare the word count of your raw server response against what you see in a rendered browser. If the raw HTML has a small fraction of the words, headings and structured data that the rendered page shows, that's the signature of a JS-rendering gap. CiteRank's <a href="/">LLM Access Check</a> automates exactly this comparison — fetching your page as each AI crawler and showing you precisely what they can and can't read, element by element.</p>

      <h2>The fix</h2>
      <p>Server-side rendering (SSR) or static generation for the content that matters — title, price, description, key specs, reviews — solves this at the source. You don't need to abandon a JavaScript framework; you need the crawlable content to exist in the initial HTML response, with JavaScript layered on top for interactivity, not as the sole delivery mechanism for the substance of the page.</p>

      <div class="post-cta">
        <a href="/" class="btn-primary">Check your page free</a>
        <a href="/audit" class="btn-ghost">Run the full AEO audit</a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
